NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"pr-1909-external-review","children":[{"type":"text","value":"PR #1909 external review"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Review target: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0ba54cd50bca908348940eb2020ec4f244450dc9"},{"type":"text","value":". The supplied\nmedium-effort review named four findings, one test gap, and one broader\ncross-PR observation. This record separates the code fact from the proposed\nbehavioral consequence."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ID"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disposition"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Evidence and remaining question"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"refuted"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"waitForJoinedDemand"},{"type":"text","value":" does run on custom-collation full-source and unindexed fallback paths. A held child-demand fixture reaches both paths for Collections and Effects, then publishes the correct "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"none"},{"type":"text","value":" window. Setting the gate to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":" makes all four cells publish a matched parent that should be absent. The full-source path still needs the publication gate for an anti-join. The indexed-only restriction proposed by the review is unsafe."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"confirmed-open"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ordered Effects track published rows for every ordered source, and the classifier reads that map even outside a held repair or joined-filter window. The suggested duplicate insert for an already published key has no valid public history yet. An equal-value source update produced no D2 delta with either classifier. A second joined contributor received a distinct composite output key. A temporary comparison found no event-type divergence through 2,048 valid plain ordered Effect mutations; their public rows matched an independent window model. Provide a valid query and source sequence that sends an insert for a callback-visible key without its matching delete before changing this branch."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R3"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"confirmed-open"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The builder tracks completion of one "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DemandUpdate.ready"},{"type":"text","value":" per plan; the Effect checks the controller's pending acquisition segments. These can differ for a microtask after segment settlement. A controlled shared-source fixture held child demand for both consumers, observed neither publish before settlement, and then observed each publish the correct anti-join row. Collection callbacks arrived before the Effect callback, so reads of both consumers differed briefly between independent callbacks. No incorrect row or stuck gate appeared. A claimed gate error still needs a sequence that separates it from ordinary callback order."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R4"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fixed-now"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Removed the redundant "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hasJoinedFilterWindow()"},{"type":"text","value":" scan from the builder's flush guard. The remaining "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".some()"},{"type":"text","value":" still requires "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"waitForJoinedDemand"},{"type":"text","value":" and pending joined work."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R5"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fixed-now"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Added four oracle cells crossing Collection/Effect with custom collation/unindexed root loading and held child demand. The custom path checks one unbounded request; the unindexed path checks prefix then full-source fallback. Both check complete anti-join publication. Disabling the gate kills all four cells."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R6"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"deferred"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The broader #1907 pattern is outside this review's code target. This PR now covers the two fallback regimes named here. The "},{"type":"link","href":"pr-1907-accepted-delete-ownership.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"#1907 review record"}]},{"type":"text","value":" remains the destination for its own guard and oracle claims."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The review identified a real test gap and a redundant guard. Its proposed\nrestriction of the joined-demand gate would introduce a visible anti-join bug.\nThe Effect classifier and split demand bookkeeping remain evidence gaps rather\nthan confirmed product failures."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"follow-up-probes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Follow-up probes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For R2, a temporary test compared "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"classifyImmediateDelta"},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"classifyHeldDelta"},{"type":"text","value":" at every ordinary ordered Effect flush and failed if their\nevent types differed. It ran 64 deterministic histories of 32 valid source\nmutations each. The histories crossed insertion, deletion, replacement, filter\nmembership, order changes, and top-two displacement over five keys. After\nevery mutation, the Effect's accumulated public rows matched an independent\nfilter/sort/window recomputation. No classifier divergence occurred. The\ninstrumented ordered-work, Effect, Effect-disposal, and pagination owners also\npassed 426 tests without a divergent event type. The temporary instrumentation\nwas removed. These are sampled histories, not a proof for every query shape."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For R3, a temporary fixture gave one on-demand root and child source to a\nlive-query Collection and an Effect. It held the child's request while both\nconsumers evaluated a custom-collation "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"none"},{"type":"text","value":" filter. Both stayed empty before\nrelease and ended with only the unmatched root. The callback sequence was\nCollection, Collection, Effect; each callback's own result was correct. A read\nof both consumers during the first Collection callback saw the Effect's earlier\nstate. Their callback schedules are separate, so that observation alone does\nnot show that either pending-work definition released at an invalid point.\nThe temporary fixture was removed; no product change was justified by it."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"follow-up-review-of-demand-scope-and-wake-up","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Follow-up review of demand scope and wake-up"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The later review was checked against "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"2a6b119f0dd85370d9f179b8a942fc163f08ea13"},{"type":"text","value":".\nIt contained two new product failures and several repeat or speculative claims."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ID"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disposition"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Evidence and destination"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fixed-now"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A direct LEFT-join anti-join stopped after its first root page while a separate include demand was held. Collection and Effect both made the next cursor request before that unrelated demand settled after the gate was scoped to the joined source and its plans. Two ordered-work oracle cells retain the RED/GREEN trace."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"refuted"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A zero-change source callback can schedule another loader pass, but the loader's last page, prefix, and tie-boundary latches prevent the stated no-progress request loop. Existing underfilled joined-window oracle cells finish with bounded, distinct requests. Disabling the last-page latch makes all 20 underfilled-source cells fail at the provider-request cap."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A3"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"design-decision"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Collection still reads plan settlement; Effect still reads controller segment state. Both now use one source-scoped pending-work predicate. The distinct state owners have no demonstrated incorrect release; the earlier shared-source probe remains in R3 above."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A4"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"refuted"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Full-source custom collation still needs the joined-demand publication gate. The earlier hostile mutant made four Collection/Effect fallback cells publish an incorrect anti-join row; the custom cell checks one full-source request."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A5"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"duplicate of A1/A3"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The repeated scope concern led to the A1 fixture. The remaining state-owner question is A3."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"B1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fixed-now"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The source/plan/status gate is now one helper. The old methods were similar but had different state queries, so they were not verbatim copies. A further refactor removed Collection's per-run loader callback registry and redundant scheduler state; the resulting PR is net-negative in production lines."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"B2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"duplicate of A4"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disabling the full-source gate is the killed mutant, not a safe fix."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"B3"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"confirmed-open"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The ordinary ordered Effect classifier concern is R2 above. The prior 2,048-step differential found no legal event-type difference; a minimal divergent source history remains needed."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"B4"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"duplicate of A2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The second no-progress-loop claim adds no new path."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"B5"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fixed-now"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A held empty joined truncate replay produced no replacement delta to wake Effect. A root update stayed at its old callback-visible value after replay settled. Listening for the joined subscription's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ready"},{"type":"text","value":" transition released the delta. The ordered-work oracle retains the RED/GREEN case. Collection already has a replay-success wake-up."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"B6"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"duplicate of B1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A shared predicate removes one scope definition. The Collection scheduler refactor supplies the net-negative code reduction; Collection and Effect still own separate public publication paths."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The second review supplied the missing unrelated-demand and empty-replay\nhistories. The alleged infinite loop did not survive the no-progress check."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"code-weight-refactor","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Code-weight refactor"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The existing D2 graph and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"OrderedSourceLoader"},{"type":"text","value":" now drive ordered continuation\non every Collection graph turn, matching the Effect runner's existing schedule.\nThe Collection builder keeps one loader set per sync run and fences queued jobs\nby sync generation. The transaction scheduler already deduplicates and clears\njobs, so the builder's second per-context callback registry and clear listener\nwere removed. Collection and Effect also use one dependency-graph scheduling\nhelper rather than separate transaction scheduling implementations. The source\nloader's fixed-point latches still bound requests when a graph turn receives\nno new rows. Effect's immediate and held delta classification now share one\nclassifier; only ordered Effects pass callback-visible row state. A single\nmonotonically increasing demand generation replaces per-plan generation\nbookkeeping."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Against merge base "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"afbeb44eef48d92b6e30ae5fd2843b938ee9163c"},{"type":"text","value":", the seven\nchanged production TypeScript files contain 215 added and 331 deleted lines\n(net "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"−116 raw lines"}]},{"type":"text","value":"). Counting nonblank, noncomment diff lines gives 197\nadded and 221 deleted (net "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"−24 executable lines"}]},{"type":"text","value":"). This measure excludes\narchitecture prose, tests, and review records. It is a line-count check, not a\nclaim that each old state was redundant: the joined-source demand and public\npublication gates remain necessary for correctness."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The full "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/db/tests"},{"type":"text","value":" run passed 6,603 tests across 186 files after the\nproduction refactor. The ordered-work oracle passed 93 cells, including the\nunrelated-demand and empty-replay repairs. The scheduler test exercised 81\ncases, including repeated-alias failure ownership across falsy failures."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"ordered-continuation-and-window-follow-up","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ordered continuation and window follow-up"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Code target: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0cbc7ae0"},{"type":"text","value":" (production change "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"450b9d3c"},{"type":"text","value":"). The later CodeRabbit comment at\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"https://github.com/TanStack/db/pull/1909#discussion_r4124008382"},{"type":"text","value":" identified\nthree additional failure mechanisms. Its suggestion to run the local CodeRabbit\nCLI is separate from those findings."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ID"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disposition"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Public or state-level witness"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"CR2-1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fixed-now"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A bounded ordered repair paused for joined demand and then stopped before it refilled the public window. The ordered-work oracle failed on the missing third row before the fix. An explicit retry also lost its window generation at the same gate. The loader-state test failed on the missing retry request before the fix. Both tests pass with retained continuation and retry intent."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"CR2-2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fixed-now"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setWindow()"},{"type":"text","value":" settled while an existing joined demand still held a required root continuation. The public ordered-work oracle failed before the fix. It now holds joined and root work separately. Its resolve, failure, retirement, and stale-rejection cells pass."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"CR2-3"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fixed-now"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cursor invalidation erased an authoritative repair continuation. A later public-row deletion exposed an underfilled window before the fix. The oracle now observes the refill after joined settlement. Reset and request failure still clear the continuation."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"CR2-4"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"deferred"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"coderabbit"},{"type":"text","value":" executable is unavailable locally. The PR check will run on the pushed commit."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The follow-up also simplified Effect source ingestion and Collection graph\nscheduling. Collection now checks ordered loaders inside one fixed-point loop.\nA public-query oracle observes a synchronous root refill before its second\njoined demand. Deleting the post-loader graph step made that oracle fail at the\nsecond-demand assertion. This was an assertion failure, not a timeout or setup\nfailure. The step remains in production. The "},{"type":"link","href":"pr-1909-final-graph-loss-audit.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"field-lab loss audit"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nrecords source-supported distinctions that the short refactor summary omitted.\nIt does not treat those omissions as product regressions."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Against merge base "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"afbeb44eef48d92b6e30ae5fd2843b938ee9163c"},{"type":"text","value":", the\nseven production TypeScript files have 318 added and 431 deleted lines. Net\nproduction size is "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"−113 raw lines"}]},{"type":"text","value":". Excluding blank and comment-only diff\nlines gives 299 added and 303 deleted, or "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"−4 executable lines"}]},{"type":"text","value":". The DB\noracle campaign passed 2,627 tests across 42 files. The DB runtime suite\npassed 6,609 tests across 186 files with Vitest typechecking disabled. Package\nTypeScript, changed-file ESLint, Prettier, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"git diff --check"},{"type":"text","value":" passed."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"oracle-guide-review-for-this-follow-up","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Oracle guide review for this follow-up"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001–003:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" The ordered-work oracle names the ordered-window and\npublication contract, uses an independent filter/sort/window reference, and\nkeeps its grammar, driver, and public checkpoint visible. The loader-state\ncase is a focused state refinement of explicit retry admission."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" This follow-up adds bounded deterministic cells and makes no new\ngenerated-grammar claim. The existing generated grammar is unchanged. The\nnew cells reconstruct the named joined-demand, repair, window-move, failure,\nretirement, and refill witnesses. Multiple simultaneous joined plans and a\npublic-query failed-retry overlap remain outside this coverage, as the\ncoverage map states."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005–006:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" The driver uses public live-query Collections and Effects.\nIt checks rows, requests, window settlement, and callback batches at named\ncheckpoints. The pre-fix runs failed at those observations. The graph-step\nmutant failed at a public second-demand assertion."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" The existing important generated property runs fixed and random\ncampaigns with direct seed/path replay. These follow-up cells are bounded\ndeterministic controls, so they add no separate generated campaign."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008–009:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" The reference model gains no state or new vocabulary.\nWindow participants in production remain distinct from reference rows."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Each new async control resolves its held promises during cleanup.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withHistoryCleanup"},{"type":"text","value":" retains the primary assertion and reports cleanup\nfailures separately."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" No new shared semantic fault hypothesis requires a second\nformulation in this follow-up. The independent filter/sort/window reference\nremains the primary result model. Remote relation hints remain a separate\nfeature."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" This versioned section records outcomes for ORC-001 through\nORC-011 against the code target and names the remaining in-scope witnesses."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The repaired claim covers finite local joined-filter windows, the named\njoined-demand and window-move histories, Collection and Effect public rows,\nand the specified settlement checkpoints. It does not claim all legal demand\ninterleavings. The coverage map owns the two remaining witnesses above."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"shipped-byte-consolidation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Shipped-byte consolidation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Code target: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"b69c189c"},{"type":"text","value":". Three independent scouts inspected graph scheduling,\nordered demand, Effect state, package exports, and dependency drag. They\nmeasured proposed cuts in isolated builds before the final edit. The chosen\nchanges remove two unused scheduler extension paths, two redundant Effect\nfields, and unused Collection scheduler overrides. The core scheduler and D2\ngraph remain necessary for variable query dependencies and coherent\npublication."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Scheduler.onClear()"},{"type":"text","value":" had no production caller, and no production dependency\nimplemented "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hasPendingGraphRun()"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Scheduler"},{"type":"text","value":" is absent from the package root\nexports. The change retires those internal extension contracts. A direct\nunsupported source import can no longer attach a clear listener or provide an\nout-of-queue pending dependency. The scheduler test now checks queued-job\ncancellation, reuse of a cleared context, prerequisite requeue, exact failure\nidentity, and later successful work. Existing Collection and Effect tests still\ncheck coherent public batches, rollback, stale settlement, and loader failure.\nThe architecture now says that only queued jobs block scheduler dependencies.\nThe "},{"type":"link","href":"pr-1909-code-weight-loss-audit.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"second field-lab loss audit"}]},{"type":"text","value":" preserves\nsource details omitted by this short reduction."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The byte assay built the same package path with Vite 7.3.2 "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"--minify"},{"type":"text","value":", then\nbundled selected ESM exports with esbuild 0.27.7. It used gzip level 9 and\nBrotli quality 11. Every row compares the identical absolute path and toolchain.\nValues are bytes; negative deltas save bytes."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,"right","right"],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Consumer import"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Change from prior PR head: min / gzip / Brotli"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Final PR against merge base: min / gzip / Brotli"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Full "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/db"},{"type":"text","value":" index"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"−484 / −112 / −44"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"+93 / −75 / −330"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createLiveQueryCollection"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"−325 / −81 / −113"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"+638 / +44 / +19"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createEffect"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"−457 / −97 / −244"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"+318 / +104 / −71"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both query exports"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"−489 / −106 / +8"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"+89 / −58 / +118"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The named query imports remain slightly larger than the merge base under some\nbyte measures. The full import has fewer compressed bytes but 93 more minified\nbytes. These receipts do not establish a universal net-negative shipped size.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/pacer-lite"},{"type":"text","value":" contributes no bytes to the measured query imports, so\nthe scouts found no query-path dependency drag to remove. The remaining\noptional scheduler dependency argument saved only 15 gzip bytes in a trial and\nwould retire another internal test contract; it was left intact."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The DB oracle campaign passed 2,627 tests across 42 files with no type errors.\nThe full DB runtime suite passed 6,605 tests across 186 files with Vitest\ntypechecking disabled. Package TypeScript, changed-file ESLint, Prettier, and\ndiff checks passed. Against the merge base, production TypeScript is "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"−156\nraw lines"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"−35 nonblank, noncomment lines"}]},{"type":"text","value":". This count includes the\nscheduler file; it excludes tests and documentation."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"xhigh-review-of-ordered-callbacks-and-code-weight","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Xhigh review of ordered callbacks and code weight"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"External review target: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"80421c8"},{"type":"text","value":". Evaluation started at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dd10cb4"},{"type":"text","value":"; the\nverified follow-up is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"b445bd13"},{"type":"text","value":" (production fix "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1a1b0952"},{"type":"text","value":"). The raw review\nnamed two behavior findings, one duplication suggestion, two lower-severity\nfindings, two oracle gaps, and broader preservation/weight claims. Its reported\nfresh-worktree and architecture-reading checks are provenance, not product\nevidence. The entries below retain those claims separately."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ID"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Claim and technical verdict"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disposition, action, and durable destination"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"X1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scheduler clear, abort, and generation fencing preserve behavior after removal of pending graph bookkeeping and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onClear"},{"type":"text","value":". The scheduler tests and earlier loss audit support the named paths. They do not prove every possible history or absence of an external deep import."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"already-fixed"},{"type":"text","value":"; no further change. The scheduler tests and "},{"type":"link","href":"pr-1909-code-weight-loss-audit.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"code-weight loss audit"}]},{"type":"text","value":" retain the exact internal behavior removed."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"X2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Removing the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sentChanges > 0"},{"type":"text","value":" loader guard and always checking "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"loadMore"},{"type":"text","value":" is intentional. The architecture assigns fixed-point control to loader latches and requires graph work after synchronous loader writes."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"already-fixed"},{"type":"text","value":"; the architecture and ordered-work oracle own this boundary and its killed post-loader-step mutant."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"X3"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An ordered "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"skipInitial"},{"type":"text","value":" Effect completed its initial phase while joined subset demand was pending, then emitted an initial "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enter"},{"type":"text","value":" after demand settled. A controlled child promise reproduced this on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dd10cb4"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fixed-now"},{"type":"text","value":"; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"canCompleteInitialLoad"},{"type":"text","value":" now checks the same joined-work condition as callback flush. The ordered-work oracle holds child demand, asserts no callback before or after settlement, then observes a later update."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"X4"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An unprojected order-key change allegedly stopped emitting an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"update"},{"type":"text","value":" because of the classifier merge. The exact public probe emitted "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"no update"}]},{"type":"text","value":" with both current code and the reviewed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"80421c8"},{"type":"text","value":" Effect source. Ordered repair selected callback-visible classification in both versions. The extra "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"previousValue"},{"type":"text","value":" claim has no observable event in this trace."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"refuted"},{"type":"text","value":" for the stated regression; no classifier change. The earlier R2 entry above retains the separate question of whether another valid ordinary ordered history changes event type or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"previousValue"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"X5"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Removed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"info.sourceId === key"},{"type":"text","value":" guards were redundant: compiler order info is stored under its own source ID. The review's claim that no other dead parameter exists is an unbounded inventory claim, not established by this check."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"already-fixed"},{"type":"text","value":" for the guards; compiler construction and ordered-query tests are the durable evidence. No exhaustive dead-code claim is adopted."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"X6"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The removed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"loadMoreCallbackSymbol"},{"type":"text","value":" dedup layer is covered by scheduler coalescing and ordered-loader request latches in the named tests."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"already-fixed"},{"type":"text","value":"; scheduler and ordered-work owners retain the law."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"X7"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The classifier merge is claimed behavior-preserving. X4 does not refute it: that alleged old event did not exist. The earlier R2 differential found no divergence in its sampled histories, but no universal equivalence follows."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deferred"},{"type":"text","value":"; the earlier R2 entry in this review record owns a still-missing valid divergent history, if one exists. No speculative classifier state was retained."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"X8"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Removed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pendingGraphRuns"},{"type":"text","value":" bookkeeping is claimed safe. This repeats X1's scheduler part."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"duplicate"},{"type":"text","value":" of X1; its distinct callback-accumulation detail remains in the code-weight loss audit."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"X9"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The joined-filter pending predicate is duplicated across Effect and Collection; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hasJoinedFilterWindow"},{"type":"text","value":" contains only the source-existence half, so “triplicated verbatim” is inaccurate. Effect now reuses one local predicate for flush and initial completion. A shared exported helper increased minified and gzip bytes in the same-path consumer builds, so the cross-engine helper was removed."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deferred"},{"type":"text","value":"; this record preserves the remaining cross-engine duplication. Revisit only with a measured byte win or a new semantic reason to share the state-owner boundary."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"X10"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A ready transition from any source caused an extra joined-filter graph schedule. A deterministic scheduler spy saw two calls for root readiness with the old condition and one with the source identity guard. Joined-source readiness still wakes the gate."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fixed-now"},{"type":"text","value":"; ordered-work oracle work-counter cell rejects the broad-condition mutant at its call-count assertion."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"X11"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"failDemand"},{"type":"text","value":" leaves an active plan unsettled after rejection. Source inspection confirms that state, but "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"transitionToError"},{"type":"text","value":" also makes the live query terminal, and sync teardown clears the plan. Marking the plan settled alone would not guarantee future recovery because subscription status is another pending-work input."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deferred"},{"type":"text","value":"; "},{"type":"link","href":"../oracle-coverage.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"coverage map"}]},{"type":"text","value":" names the missing nonterminal per-demand recovery contract and its required plan/subscription reconciliation. No current public wedge was reproduced."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"X12"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The oracle lacked "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"skipInitial"},{"type":"text","value":" crossed with pending joined demand."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fixed-now"},{"type":"text","value":", linked to X3; the ordered-work oracle now contains the held-demand witness."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"X13"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The oracle lacked an unprojected order-key reorder callback check. The review's expected "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"update"},{"type":"text","value":" conflicts with the reviewed Effect path and the architecture's held-repair value rule."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"design-decision"},{"type":"text","value":"; if position-only changes should produce callbacks, define that public policy first and give the ordered-work owner an independent event law. No present regression is claimed."}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"red-green-and-byte-evidence","children":[{"type":"text","value":"RED/GREEN and byte evidence"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Before the fix, the new public Effect witness received "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enter"},{"type":"text","value":" after the held\nchild load resolved. With "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1a1b0952"},{"type":"text","value":", it received no initial callback and did\nreceive a later "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"update"},{"type":"text","value":". The ready-work witness observed two root-ready graph\nschedules under a temporary broad-condition mutant; the source-specific guard\nreduced that to one while the joined-ready transition still scheduled its\nwork. Both mutants failed assertions at the intended checkpoints. The\nreorder witness failed its proposed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"update"},{"type":"text","value":" expectation on both "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dd10cb4"},{"type":"text","value":"\nand the reviewed Effect source from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"80421c8"},{"type":"text","value":"; this is a refutation of the\nreview's old-versus-new causal claim, not proof of every callback policy."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The final DB oracle campaign passed "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2,629 tests across 42 files"}]},{"type":"text","value":", with no\nVitest type errors. The focused ordered-work, scheduler, and Effect suites\npassed 250 tests. Package TypeScript, changed-file ESLint, Prettier, and diff\nchecks passed. The test-only cleanup follow-up retained primary assertion\nfailures while releasing held work. The production diff from merge base\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"afbeb44e"},{"type":"text","value":" remains "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"−153 raw TypeScript lines"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In the Vite 7.3.2 and esbuild 0.27.7 same-path trial, the proposed shared\nhelper cost 37 more minified and 20 more gzip bytes in the full index than\nthe selected local reuse. It cost 108/25 more minified/gzip bytes for the\nCollection import and 38/17 more for the Effect import. It saved 108 Brotli\nbytes in the full index but added 15 and 9 Brotli bytes in the two named\nimports. The selected local reuse plus behavior fixes changed the measured\nconsumer imports against "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dd10cb4"},{"type":"text","value":" as follows:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,"right","right","right"],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Consumer import"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Minified"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Gzip"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Brotli"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Full index"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"+41 B"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"+7 B"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"−74 B"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createLiveQueryCollection"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"−65 B"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"−5 B"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"+83 B"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createEffect"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"+106 B"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"+18 B"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"+107 B"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"These are incremental build differences from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dd10cb4"},{"type":"text","value":", not a claim that\nevery PR entry point is net smaller than the merge base."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"oracle-guide-review","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Oracle guide review"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001–003:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" The architecture states the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"skipInitial"},{"type":"text","value":" boundary; the\nordered-work oracle keeps the contract, plain-result model, scenario grammar,\nreal Collection/Effect driver, and callback checkpoint visible. The work\ncounter is a partial oracle for scheduling calls, not public row truth."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004 and ORC-007:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" These are deterministic controls. The existing\ngenerated grammar and fixed/random campaigns are unchanged and both ran in\nthe 2,629-test oracle campaign."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005–006:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"childLoads > 0"},{"type":"text","value":" proves the held joined source was reached.\nThe recorded callback list can contain an illegal initial "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enter"},{"type":"text","value":". The\npre-fix run rejected that event; the broad-condition mutant failed the\nscheduler count after a real root ready transition."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008–009:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" The reference gains no model state. The new checks use the\nglossary's Collection, joined demand, readiness, and publication terms."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withHistoryCleanup"},{"type":"text","value":" releases the held promise and all\nCollections while preserving the primary failure and separate cleanup\ndiagnostics."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" No plausible shared semantic fault between the existing plain\nrow reference and the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"skipInitial"},{"type":"text","value":" callback law was named. The independent\ncallback assertion uses the established "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"skipInitial"},{"type":"text","value":" contract."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" This versioned section records the two RED controls, the GREEN\nrun, the exact code target, the bounded claim, and the remaining cells.\nMultiple simultaneous joined plans during a window move and a public-query\nfailed-retry overlap remain in the coverage map. Nonterminal per-demand\nrecovery is a separate undefined contract."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"final-loss-audit-and-reviewer-assessment","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Final loss audit and reviewer assessment"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The raw review was reread after the fix. Its 13 recorded items reconcile as\n"},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"3 fixed-now + 4 already-fixed + 1 duplicate + 1 refuted + 3 deferred +\n1 design-decision = 13"}]},{"type":"text","value":". The only unresolved behavioral evidence gap is the\nearlier R2 ordinary-classifier concern, now linked from X7; the stated X4\nreorder trace does not supply it. X11 remains a future recovery design issue."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The reviewer showed useful depth on X3 and X10, including a concrete async\nsequence and a work-specific source comparison. Accuracy and prioritization\nwere mixed: X4's confident regression claim failed on its own old path, X9\noverstated duplication and byte savings, and X11 correctly identified a\nlatent state mismatch without showing a current product failure. The review\nhas moderate signal-to-noise and good test-gap instincts. "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hire for code\nreview with an executable-verification requirement for blocking behavioral\nclaims; do not use this review alone as a merge gate."}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"pr-1909-external-review","text":"PR #1909 external review","level":1},{"id":"follow-up-probes","text":"Follow-up probes","level":2},{"id":"follow-up-review-of-demand-scope-and-wake-up","text":"Follow-up review of demand scope and wake-up","level":2},{"id":"code-weight-refactor","text":"Code-weight refactor","level":2},{"id":"ordered-continuation-and-window-follow-up","text":"Ordered continuation and window follow-up","level":2},{"id":"oracle-guide-review-for-this-follow-up","text":"Oracle guide review for this follow-up","level":3},{"id":"shipped-byte-consolidation","text":"Shipped-byte consolidation","level":2},{"id":"xhigh-review-of-ordered-callbacks-and-code-weight","text":"Xhigh review of ordered callbacks and code weight","level":2},{"id":"red-green-and-byte-evidence","text":"RED/GREEN and byte evidence","level":3},{"id":"oracle-guide-review","text":"Oracle guide review","level":3},{"id":"final-loss-audit-and-reviewer-assessment","text":"Final loss audit and reviewer assessment","level":3}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"# PR #1909 external review\n\nReview target: `0ba54cd50bca908348940eb2020ec4f244450dc9`. The supplied\nmedium-effort review named four findings, one test gap, and one broader\ncross-PR observation. This record separates the code fact from the proposed\nbehavioral consequence.\n\n| ID | Disposition | Evidence and remaining question |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| R1 | refuted | `waitForJoinedDemand` does run on custom-collation full-source and unindexed fallback paths. A held child-demand fixture reaches both paths for Collections and Effects, then publishes the correct `none` window. Setting the gate to `false` makes all four cells publish a matched parent that should be absent. The full-source path still needs the publication gate for an anti-join. The indexed-only restriction proposed by the review is unsafe. |\n| R2 | confirmed-open | Ordered Effects track published rows for every ordered source, and the classifier reads that map even outside a held repair or joined-filter window. The suggested duplicate insert for an already published key has no valid public history yet. An equal-value source update produced no D2 delta with either classifier. A second joined contributor received a distinct composite output key. A temporary comparison found no event-type divergence through 2,048 valid plain ordered Effect mutations; their public rows matched an independent window model. Provide a valid query and source sequence that sends an insert for a callback-visible key without its matching delete before changing this branch. |\n| R3 | confirmed-open | The builder tracks completion of one `DemandUpdate.ready` per plan; the Effect checks the controller's pending acquisition segments. These can differ for a microtask after segment settlement. A controlled shared-source fixture held child demand for both consumers, observed neither publish before settlement, and then observed each publish the correct anti-join row. Collection callbacks arrived before the Effect callback, so reads of both consumers differed briefly between independent callbacks. No incorrect row or stuck gate appeared. A claimed gate error still needs a sequence that separates it from ordinary callback order. |\n| R4 | fixed-now | Removed the redundant `hasJoinedFilterWindow()` scan from the builder's flush guard. The remaining `.some()` still requires `waitForJoinedDemand` and pending joined work. |\n| R5 | fixed-now | Added four oracle cells crossing Collection/Effect with custom collation/unindexed root loading and held child demand. The custom path checks one unbounded request; the unindexed path checks prefix then full-source fallback. Both check complete anti-join publication. Disabling the gate kills all four cells. |\n| R6 | deferred | The broader #1907 pattern is outside this review's code target. This PR now covers the two fallback regimes named here. The [#1907 review record](pr-1907-accepted-delete-ownership.md) remains the destination for its own guard and oracle claims. |\n\nThe review identified a real test gap and a redundant guard. Its proposed\nrestriction of the joined-demand gate would introduce a visible anti-join bug.\nThe Effect classifier and split demand bookkeeping remain evidence gaps rather\nthan confirmed product failures.\n\n## Follow-up probes\n\nFor R2, a temporary test compared `classifyImmediateDelta` and\n`classifyHeldDelta` at every ordinary ordered Effect flush and failed if their\nevent types differed. It ran 64 deterministic histories of 32 valid source\nmutations each. The histories crossed insertion, deletion, replacement, filter\nmembership, order changes, and top-two displacement over five keys. After\nevery mutation, the Effect's accumulated public rows matched an independent\nfilter/sort/window recomputation. No classifier divergence occurred. The\ninstrumented ordered-work, Effect, Effect-disposal, and pagination owners also\npassed 426 tests without a divergent event type. The temporary instrumentation\nwas removed. These are sampled histories, not a proof for every query shape.\n\nFor R3, a temporary fixture gave one on-demand root and child source to a\nlive-query Collection and an Effect. It held the child's request while both\nconsumers evaluated a custom-collation `none` filter. Both stayed empty before\nrelease and ended with only the unmatched root. The callback sequence was\nCollection, Collection, Effect; each callback's own result was correct. A read\nof both consumers during the first Collection callback saw the Effect's earlier\nstate. Their callback schedules are separate, so that observation alone does\nnot show that either pending-work definition released at an invalid point.\nThe temporary fixture was removed; no product change was justified by it.\n\n## Follow-up review of demand scope and wake-up\n\nThe later review was checked against `2a6b119f0dd85370d9f179b8a942fc163f08ea13`.\nIt contained two new product failures and several repeat or speculative claims.\n\n| ID | Disposition | Evidence and destination |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| A1 | fixed-now | A direct LEFT-join anti-join stopped after its first root page while a separate include demand was held. Collection and Effect both made the next cursor request before that unrelated demand settled after the gate was scoped to the joined source and its plans. Two ordered-work oracle cells retain the RED/GREEN trace. |\n| A2 | refuted | A zero-change source callback can schedule another loader pass, but the loader's last page, prefix, and tie-boundary latches prevent the stated no-progress request loop. Existing underfilled joined-window oracle cells finish with bounded, distinct requests. Disabling the last-page latch makes all 20 underfilled-source cells fail at the provider-request cap. |\n| A3 | design-decision | Collection still reads plan settlement; Effect still reads controller segment state. Both now use one source-scoped pending-work predicate. The distinct state owners have no demonstrated incorrect release; the earlier shared-source probe remains in R3 above. |\n| A4 | refuted | Full-source custom collation still needs the joined-demand publication gate. The earlier hostile mutant made four Collection/Effect fallback cells publish an incorrect anti-join row; the custom cell checks one full-source request. |\n| A5 | duplicate of A1/A3 | The repeated scope concern led to the A1 fixture. The remaining state-owner question is A3. |\n| B1 | fixed-now | The source/plan/status gate is now one helper. The old methods were similar but had different state queries, so they were not verbatim copies. A further refactor removed Collection's per-run loader callback registry and redundant scheduler state; the resulting PR is net-negative in production lines. |\n| B2 | duplicate of A4 | Disabling the full-source gate is the killed mutant, not a safe fix. |\n| B3 | confirmed-open | The ordinary ordered Effect classifier concern is R2 above. The prior 2,048-step differential found no legal event-type difference; a minimal divergent source history remains needed. |\n| B4 | duplicate of A2 | The second no-progress-loop claim adds no new path. |\n| B5 | fixed-now | A held empty joined truncate replay produced no replacement delta to wake Effect. A root update stayed at its old callback-visible value after replay settled. Listening for the joined subscription's `ready` transition released the delta. The ordered-work oracle retains the RED/GREEN case. Collection already has a replay-success wake-up. |\n| B6 | duplicate of B1 | A shared predicate removes one scope definition. The Collection scheduler refactor supplies the net-negative code reduction; Collection and Effect still own separate public publication paths. |\n\nThe second review supplied the missing unrelated-demand and empty-replay\nhistories. The alleged infinite loop did not survive the no-progress check.\n\n## Code-weight refactor\n\nThe existing D2 graph and `OrderedSourceLoader` now drive ordered continuation\non every Collection graph turn, matching the Effect runner's existing schedule.\nThe Collection builder keeps one loader set per sync run and fences queued jobs\nby sync generation. The transaction scheduler already deduplicates and clears\njobs, so the builder's second per-context callback registry and clear listener\nwere removed. Collection and Effect also use one dependency-graph scheduling\nhelper rather than separate transaction scheduling implementations. The source\nloader's fixed-point latches still bound requests when a graph turn receives\nno new rows. Effect's immediate and held delta classification now share one\nclassifier; only ordered Effects pass callback-visible row state. A single\nmonotonically increasing demand generation replaces per-plan generation\nbookkeeping.\n\nAgainst merge base `afbeb44eef48d92b6e30ae5fd2843b938ee9163c`, the seven\nchanged production TypeScript files contain 215 added and 331 deleted lines\n(net **−116 raw lines**). Counting nonblank, noncomment diff lines gives 197\nadded and 221 deleted (net **−24 executable lines**). This measure excludes\narchitecture prose, tests, and review records. It is a line-count check, not a\nclaim that each old state was redundant: the joined-source demand and public\npublication gates remain necessary for correctness.\n\nThe full `packages/db/tests` run passed 6,603 tests across 186 files after the\nproduction refactor. The ordered-work oracle passed 93 cells, including the\nunrelated-demand and empty-replay repairs. The scheduler test exercised 81\ncases, including repeated-alias failure ownership across falsy failures.\n\n## Ordered continuation and window follow-up\n\nCode target: `0cbc7ae0` (production change `450b9d3c`). The later CodeRabbit comment at\n`https://github.com/TanStack/db/pull/1909#discussion_r4124008382` identified\nthree additional failure mechanisms. Its suggestion to run the local CodeRabbit\nCLI is separate from those findings.\n\n| ID | Disposition | Public or state-level witness |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| CR2-1 | fixed-now | A bounded ordered repair paused for joined demand and then stopped before it refilled the public window. The ordered-work oracle failed on the missing third row before the fix. An explicit retry also lost its window generation at the same gate. The loader-state test failed on the missing retry request before the fix. Both tests pass with retained continuation and retry intent. |\n| CR2-2 | fixed-now | `setWindow()` settled while an existing joined demand still held a required root continuation. The public ordered-work oracle failed before the fix. It now holds joined and root work separately. Its resolve, failure, retirement, and stale-rejection cells pass. |\n| CR2-3 | fixed-now | Cursor invalidation erased an authoritative repair continuation. A later public-row deletion exposed an underfilled window before the fix. The oracle now observes the refill after joined settlement. Reset and request failure still clear the continuation. |\n| CR2-4 | deferred | The `coderabbit` executable is unavailable locally. The PR check will run on the pushed commit. |\n\nThe follow-up also simplified Effect source ingestion and Collection graph\nscheduling. Collection now checks ordered loaders inside one fixed-point loop.\nA public-query oracle observes a synchronous root refill before its second\njoined demand. Deleting the post-loader graph step made that oracle fail at the\nsecond-demand assertion. This was an assertion failure, not a timeout or setup\nfailure. The step remains in production. The [field-lab loss audit](pr-1909-final-graph-loss-audit.md)\nrecords source-supported distinctions that the short refactor summary omitted.\nIt does not treat those omissions as product regressions.\n\nAgainst merge base `afbeb44eef48d92b6e30ae5fd2843b938ee9163c`, the\nseven production TypeScript files have 318 added and 431 deleted lines. Net\nproduction size is **−113 raw lines**. Excluding blank and comment-only diff\nlines gives 299 added and 303 deleted, or **−4 executable lines**. The DB\noracle campaign passed 2,627 tests across 42 files. The DB runtime suite\npassed 6,609 tests across 186 files with Vitest typechecking disabled. Package\nTypeScript, changed-file ESLint, Prettier, and `git diff --check` passed.\n\n### Oracle guide review for this follow-up\n\n- **ORC-001–003:** The ordered-work oracle names the ordered-window and\n  publication contract, uses an independent filter/sort/window reference, and\n  keeps its grammar, driver, and public checkpoint visible. The loader-state\n  case is a focused state refinement of explicit retry admission.\n- **ORC-004:** This follow-up adds bounded deterministic cells and makes no new\n  generated-grammar claim. The existing generated grammar is unchanged. The\n  new cells reconstruct the named joined-demand, repair, window-move, failure,\n  retirement, and refill witnesses. Multiple simultaneous joined plans and a\n  public-query failed-retry overlap remain outside this coverage, as the\n  coverage map states.\n- **ORC-005–006:** The driver uses public live-query Collections and Effects.\n  It checks rows, requests, window settlement, and callback batches at named\n  checkpoints. The pre-fix runs failed at those observations. The graph-step\n  mutant failed at a public second-demand assertion.\n- **ORC-007:** The existing important generated property runs fixed and random\n  campaigns with direct seed/path replay. These follow-up cells are bounded\n  deterministic controls, so they add no separate generated campaign.\n- **ORC-008–009:** The reference model gains no state or new vocabulary.\n  Window participants in production remain distinct from reference rows.\n- **ORC-010:** Each new async control resolves its held promises during cleanup.\n  `withHistoryCleanup` retains the primary assertion and reports cleanup\n  failures separately.\n- **ORC-011:** No new shared semantic fault hypothesis requires a second\n  formulation in this follow-up. The independent filter/sort/window reference\n  remains the primary result model. Remote relation hints remain a separate\n  feature.\n- **ORC-012:** This versioned section records outcomes for ORC-001 through\n  ORC-011 against the code target and names the remaining in-scope witnesses.\n\nThe repaired claim covers finite local joined-filter windows, the named\njoined-demand and window-move histories, Collection and Effect public rows,\nand the specified settlement checkpoints. It does not claim all legal demand\ninterleavings. The coverage map owns the two remaining witnesses above.\n\n## Shipped-byte consolidation\n\nCode target: `b69c189c`. Three independent scouts inspected graph scheduling,\nordered demand, Effect state, package exports, and dependency drag. They\nmeasured proposed cuts in isolated builds before the final edit. The chosen\nchanges remove two unused scheduler extension paths, two redundant Effect\nfields, and unused Collection scheduler overrides. The core scheduler and D2\ngraph remain necessary for variable query dependencies and coherent\npublication.\n\n`Scheduler.onClear()` had no production caller, and no production dependency\nimplemented `hasPendingGraphRun()`. `Scheduler` is absent from the package root\nexports. The change retires those internal extension contracts. A direct\nunsupported source import can no longer attach a clear listener or provide an\nout-of-queue pending dependency. The scheduler test now checks queued-job\ncancellation, reuse of a cleared context, prerequisite requeue, exact failure\nidentity, and later successful work. Existing Collection and Effect tests still\ncheck coherent public batches, rollback, stale settlement, and loader failure.\nThe architecture now says that only queued jobs block scheduler dependencies.\nThe [second field-lab loss audit](pr-1909-code-weight-loss-audit.md) preserves\nsource details omitted by this short reduction.\n\nThe byte assay built the same package path with Vite 7.3.2 `--minify`, then\nbundled selected ESM exports with esbuild 0.27.7. It used gzip level 9 and\nBrotli quality 11. Every row compares the identical absolute path and toolchain.\nValues are bytes; negative deltas save bytes.\n\n| Consumer import | Change from prior PR head: min / gzip / Brotli | Final PR against merge base: min / gzip / Brotli |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Full `@tanstack/db` index | −484 / −112 / −44 | +93 / −75 / −330 |\n| `createLiveQueryCollection` | −325 / −81 / −113 | +638 / +44 / +19 |\n| `createEffect` | −457 / −97 / −244 | +318 / +104 / −71 |\n| Both query exports | −489 / −106 / +8 | +89 / −58 / +118 |\n\nThe named query imports remain slightly larger than the merge base under some\nbyte measures. The full import has fewer compressed bytes but 93 more minified\nbytes. These receipts do not establish a universal net-negative shipped size.\n`@tanstack/pacer-lite` contributes no bytes to the measured query imports, so\nthe scouts found no query-path dependency drag to remove. The remaining\noptional scheduler dependency argument saved only 15 gzip bytes in a trial and\nwould retire another internal test contract; it was left intact.\n\nThe DB oracle campaign passed 2,627 tests across 42 files with no type errors.\nThe full DB runtime suite passed 6,605 tests across 186 files with Vitest\ntypechecking disabled. Package TypeScript, changed-file ESLint, Prettier, and\ndiff checks passed. Against the merge base, production TypeScript is **−156\nraw lines** and **−35 nonblank, noncomment lines**. This count includes the\nscheduler file; it excludes tests and documentation.\n\n## Xhigh review of ordered callbacks and code weight\n\nExternal review target: `80421c8`. Evaluation started at `dd10cb4`; the\nverified follow-up is `b445bd13` (production fix `1a1b0952`). The raw review\nnamed two behavior findings, one duplication suggestion, two lower-severity\nfindings, two oracle gaps, and broader preservation/weight claims. Its reported\nfresh-worktree and architecture-reading checks are provenance, not product\nevidence. The entries below retain those claims separately.\n\n| ID | Claim and technical verdict | Disposition, action, and durable destination |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| X1 | Scheduler clear, abort, and generation fencing preserve behavior after removal of pending graph bookkeeping and `onClear`. The scheduler tests and earlier loss audit support the named paths. They do not prove every possible history or absence of an external deep import. | `already-fixed`; no further change. The scheduler tests and [code-weight loss audit](pr-1909-code-weight-loss-audit.md) retain the exact internal behavior removed. |\n| X2 | Removing the `sentChanges > 0` loader guard and always checking `loadMore` is intentional. The architecture assigns fixed-point control to loader latches and requires graph work after synchronous loader writes. | `already-fixed`; the architecture and ordered-work oracle own this boundary and its killed post-loader-step mutant. |\n| X3 | An ordered `skipInitial` Effect completed its initial phase while joined subset demand was pending, then emitted an initial `enter` after demand settled. A controlled child promise reproduced this on `dd10cb4`. | `fixed-now`; `canCompleteInitialLoad` now checks the same joined-work condition as callback flush. The ordered-work oracle holds child demand, asserts no callback before or after settlement, then observes a later update. |\n| X4 | An unprojected order-key change allegedly stopped emitting an `update` because of the classifier merge. The exact public probe emitted **no update** with both current code and the reviewed `80421c8` Effect source. Ordered repair selected callback-visible classification in both versions. The extra `previousValue` claim has no observable event in this trace. | `refuted` for the stated regression; no classifier change. The earlier R2 entry above retains the separate question of whether another valid ordinary ordered history changes event type or `previousValue`. |\n| X5 | Removed `info.sourceId === key` guards were redundant: compiler order info is stored under its own source ID. The review's claim that no other dead parameter exists is an unbounded inventory claim, not established by this check. | `already-fixed` for the guards; compiler construction and ordered-query tests are the durable evidence. No exhaustive dead-code claim is adopted. |\n| X6 | The removed `loadMoreCallbackSymbol` dedup layer is covered by scheduler coalescing and ordered-loader request latches in the named tests. | `already-fixed`; scheduler and ordered-work owners retain the law. |\n| X7 | The classifier merge is claimed behavior-preserving. X4 does not refute it: that alleged old event did not exist. The earlier R2 differential found no divergence in its sampled histories, but no universal equivalence follows. | `deferred`; the earlier R2 entry in this review record owns a still-missing valid divergent history, if one exists. No speculative classifier state was retained. |\n| X8 | Removed `pendingGraphRuns` bookkeeping is claimed safe. This repeats X1's scheduler part. | `duplicate` of X1; its distinct callback-accumulation detail remains in the code-weight loss audit. |\n| X9 | The joined-filter pending predicate is duplicated across Effect and Collection; `hasJoinedFilterWindow` contains only the source-existence half, so “triplicated verbatim” is inaccurate. Effect now reuses one local predicate for flush and initial completion. A shared exported helper increased minified and gzip bytes in the same-path consumer builds, so the cross-engine helper was removed. | `deferred`; this record preserves the remaining cross-engine duplication. Revisit only with a measured byte win or a new semantic reason to share the state-owner boundary. |\n| X10 | A ready transition from any source caused an extra joined-filter graph schedule. A deterministic scheduler spy saw two calls for root readiness with the old condition and one with the source identity guard. Joined-source readiness still wakes the gate. | `fixed-now`; ordered-work oracle work-counter cell rejects the broad-condition mutant at its call-count assertion. |\n| X11 | `failDemand` leaves an active plan unsettled after rejection. Source inspection confirms that state, but `transitionToError` also makes the live query terminal, and sync teardown clears the plan. Marking the plan settled alone would not guarantee future recovery because subscription status is another pending-work input. | `deferred`; [coverage map](../oracle-coverage.md) names the missing nonterminal per-demand recovery contract and its required plan/subscription reconciliation. No current public wedge was reproduced. |\n| X12 | The oracle lacked `skipInitial` crossed with pending joined demand. | `fixed-now`, linked to X3; the ordered-work oracle now contains the held-demand witness. |\n| X13 | The oracle lacked an unprojected order-key reorder callback check. The review's expected `update` conflicts with the reviewed Effect path and the architecture's held-repair value rule. | `design-decision`; if position-only changes should produce callbacks, define that public policy first and give the ordered-work owner an independent event law. No present regression is claimed. |\n\n### RED/GREEN and byte evidence\n\nBefore the fix, the new public Effect witness received `enter` after the held\nchild load resolved. With `1a1b0952`, it received no initial callback and did\nreceive a later `update`. The ready-work witness observed two root-ready graph\nschedules under a temporary broad-condition mutant; the source-specific guard\nreduced that to one while the joined-ready transition still scheduled its\nwork. Both mutants failed assertions at the intended checkpoints. The\nreorder witness failed its proposed `update` expectation on both `dd10cb4`\nand the reviewed Effect source from `80421c8`; this is a refutation of the\nreview's old-versus-new causal claim, not proof of every callback policy.\n\nThe final DB oracle campaign passed **2,629 tests across 42 files**, with no\nVitest type errors. The focused ordered-work, scheduler, and Effect suites\npassed 250 tests. Package TypeScript, changed-file ESLint, Prettier, and diff\nchecks passed. The test-only cleanup follow-up retained primary assertion\nfailures while releasing held work. The production diff from merge base\n`afbeb44e` remains **−153 raw TypeScript lines**.\n\nIn the Vite 7.3.2 and esbuild 0.27.7 same-path trial, the proposed shared\nhelper cost 37 more minified and 20 more gzip bytes in the full index than\nthe selected local reuse. It cost 108/25 more minified/gzip bytes for the\nCollection import and 38/17 more for the Effect import. It saved 108 Brotli\nbytes in the full index but added 15 and 9 Brotli bytes in the two named\nimports. The selected local reuse plus behavior fixes changed the measured\nconsumer imports against `dd10cb4` as follows:\n\n| Consumer import | Minified | Gzip | Brotli |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Full index | +41 B | +7 B | −74 B |\n| `createLiveQueryCollection` | −65 B | −5 B | +83 B |\n| `createEffect` | +106 B | +18 B | +107 B |\n\nThese are incremental build differences from `dd10cb4`, not a claim that\nevery PR entry point is net smaller than the merge base.\n\n### Oracle guide review\n\n- **ORC-001–003:** The architecture states the `skipInitial` boundary; the\n  ordered-work oracle keeps the contract, plain-result model, scenario grammar,\n  real Collection/Effect driver, and callback checkpoint visible. The work\n  counter is a partial oracle for scheduling calls, not public row truth.\n- **ORC-004 and ORC-007:** These are deterministic controls. The existing\n  generated grammar and fixed/random campaigns are unchanged and both ran in\n  the 2,629-test oracle campaign.\n- **ORC-005–006:** `childLoads > 0` proves the held joined source was reached.\n  The recorded callback list can contain an illegal initial `enter`. The\n  pre-fix run rejected that event; the broad-condition mutant failed the\n  scheduler count after a real root ready transition.\n- **ORC-008–009:** The reference gains no model state. The new checks use the\n  glossary's Collection, joined demand, readiness, and publication terms.\n- **ORC-010:** `withHistoryCleanup` releases the held promise and all\n  Collections while preserving the primary failure and separate cleanup\n  diagnostics.\n- **ORC-011:** No plausible shared semantic fault between the existing plain\n  row reference and the `skipInitial` callback law was named. The independent\n  callback assertion uses the established `skipInitial` contract.\n- **ORC-012:** This versioned section records the two RED controls, the GREEN\n  run, the exact code target, the bounded claim, and the remaining cells.\n  Multiple simultaneous joined plans during a window move and a public-query\n  failed-retry overlap remain in the coverage map. Nonterminal per-demand\n  recovery is a separate undefined contract.\n\n### Final loss audit and reviewer assessment\n\nThe raw review was reread after the fix. Its 13 recorded items reconcile as\n**3 fixed-now + 4 already-fixed + 1 duplicate + 1 refuted + 3 deferred +\n1 design-decision = 13**. The only unresolved behavioral evidence gap is the\nearlier R2 ordinary-classifier concern, now linked from X7; the stated X4\nreorder trace does not supply it. X11 remains a future recovery design issue.\n\nThe reviewer showed useful depth on X3 and X10, including a concrete async\nsequence and a work-specific source comparison. Accuracy and prioritization\nwere mixed: X4's confident regression claim failed on its own old path, X9\noverstated duplication and byte savings, and X11 correctly identified a\nlatent state mismatch without showing a current product failure. The review\nhas moderate signal-to-noise and good test-gap instincts. **Hire for code\nreview with an executable-verification requirement for blocking behavioral\nclaims; do not use this review alone as a merge gate.**\n","originalRawMarkdown":"# PR #1909 external review\n\nReview target: `0ba54cd50bca908348940eb2020ec4f244450dc9`. The supplied\nmedium-effort review named four findings, one test gap, and one broader\ncross-PR observation. This record separates the code fact from the proposed\nbehavioral consequence.\n\n| ID | Disposition | Evidence and remaining question |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| R1 | refuted | `waitForJoinedDemand` does run on custom-collation full-source and unindexed fallback paths. A held child-demand fixture reaches both paths for Collections and Effects, then publishes the correct `none` window. Setting the gate to `false` makes all four cells publish a matched parent that should be absent. The full-source path still needs the publication gate for an anti-join. The indexed-only restriction proposed by the review is unsafe. |\n| R2 | confirmed-open | Ordered Effects track published rows for every ordered source, and the classifier reads that map even outside a held repair or joined-filter window. The suggested duplicate insert for an already published key has no valid public history yet. An equal-value source update produced no D2 delta with either classifier. A second joined contributor received a distinct composite output key. A temporary comparison found no event-type divergence through 2,048 valid plain ordered Effect mutations; their public rows matched an independent window model. Provide a valid query and source sequence that sends an insert for a callback-visible key without its matching delete before changing this branch. |\n| R3 | confirmed-open | The builder tracks completion of one `DemandUpdate.ready` per plan; the Effect checks the controller's pending acquisition segments. These can differ for a microtask after segment settlement. A controlled shared-source fixture held child demand for both consumers, observed neither publish before settlement, and then observed each publish the correct anti-join row. Collection callbacks arrived before the Effect callback, so reads of both consumers differed briefly between independent callbacks. No incorrect row or stuck gate appeared. A claimed gate error still needs a sequence that separates it from ordinary callback order. |\n| R4 | fixed-now | Removed the redundant `hasJoinedFilterWindow()` scan from the builder's flush guard. The remaining `.some()` still requires `waitForJoinedDemand` and pending joined work. |\n| R5 | fixed-now | Added four oracle cells crossing Collection/Effect with custom collation/unindexed root loading and held child demand. The custom path checks one unbounded request; the unindexed path checks prefix then full-source fallback. Both check complete anti-join publication. Disabling the gate kills all four cells. |\n| R6 | deferred | The broader #1907 pattern is outside this review's code target. This PR now covers the two fallback regimes named here. The [#1907 review record](pr-1907-accepted-delete-ownership.md) remains the destination for its own guard and oracle claims. |\n\nThe review identified a real test gap and a redundant guard. Its proposed\nrestriction of the joined-demand gate would introduce a visible anti-join bug.\nThe Effect classifier and split demand bookkeeping remain evidence gaps rather\nthan confirmed product failures.\n\n## Follow-up probes\n\nFor R2, a temporary test compared `classifyImmediateDelta` and\n`classifyHeldDelta` at every ordinary ordered Effect flush and failed if their\nevent types differed. It ran 64 deterministic histories of 32 valid source\nmutations each. The histories crossed insertion, deletion, replacement, filter\nmembership, order changes, and top-two displacement over five keys. After\nevery mutation, the Effect's accumulated public rows matched an independent\nfilter/sort/window recomputation. No classifier divergence occurred. The\ninstrumented ordered-work, Effect, Effect-disposal, and pagination owners also\npassed 426 tests without a divergent event type. The temporary instrumentation\nwas removed. These are sampled histories, not a proof for every query shape.\n\nFor R3, a temporary fixture gave one on-demand root and child source to a\nlive-query Collection and an Effect. It held the child's request while both\nconsumers evaluated a custom-collation `none` filter. Both stayed empty before\nrelease and ended with only the unmatched root. The callback sequence was\nCollection, Collection, Effect; each callback's own result was correct. A read\nof both consumers during the first Collection callback saw the Effect's earlier\nstate. Their callback schedules are separate, so that observation alone does\nnot show that either pending-work definition released at an invalid point.\nThe temporary fixture was removed; no product change was justified by it.\n\n## Follow-up review of demand scope and wake-up\n\nThe later review was checked against `2a6b119f0dd85370d9f179b8a942fc163f08ea13`.\nIt contained two new product failures and several repeat or speculative claims.\n\n| ID | Disposition | Evidence and destination |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| A1 | fixed-now | A direct LEFT-join anti-join stopped after its first root page while a separate include demand was held. Collection and Effect both made the next cursor request before that unrelated demand settled after the gate was scoped to the joined source and its plans. Two ordered-work oracle cells retain the RED/GREEN trace. |\n| A2 | refuted | A zero-change source callback can schedule another loader pass, but the loader's last page, prefix, and tie-boundary latches prevent the stated no-progress request loop. Existing underfilled joined-window oracle cells finish with bounded, distinct requests. Disabling the last-page latch makes all 20 underfilled-source cells fail at the provider-request cap. |\n| A3 | design-decision | Collection still reads plan settlement; Effect still reads controller segment state. Both now use one source-scoped pending-work predicate. The distinct state owners have no demonstrated incorrect release; the earlier shared-source probe remains in R3 above. |\n| A4 | refuted | Full-source custom collation still needs the joined-demand publication gate. The earlier hostile mutant made four Collection/Effect fallback cells publish an incorrect anti-join row; the custom cell checks one full-source request. |\n| A5 | duplicate of A1/A3 | The repeated scope concern led to the A1 fixture. The remaining state-owner question is A3. |\n| B1 | fixed-now | The source/plan/status gate is now one helper. The old methods were similar but had different state queries, so they were not verbatim copies. A further refactor removed Collection's per-run loader callback registry and redundant scheduler state; the resulting PR is net-negative in production lines. |\n| B2 | duplicate of A4 | Disabling the full-source gate is the killed mutant, not a safe fix. |\n| B3 | confirmed-open | The ordinary ordered Effect classifier concern is R2 above. The prior 2,048-step differential found no legal event-type difference; a minimal divergent source history remains needed. |\n| B4 | duplicate of A2 | The second no-progress-loop claim adds no new path. |\n| B5 | fixed-now | A held empty joined truncate replay produced no replacement delta to wake Effect. A root update stayed at its old callback-visible value after replay settled. Listening for the joined subscription's `ready` transition released the delta. The ordered-work oracle retains the RED/GREEN case. Collection already has a replay-success wake-up. |\n| B6 | duplicate of B1 | A shared predicate removes one scope definition. The Collection scheduler refactor supplies the net-negative code reduction; Collection and Effect still own separate public publication paths. |\n\nThe second review supplied the missing unrelated-demand and empty-replay\nhistories. The alleged infinite loop did not survive the no-progress check.\n\n## Code-weight refactor\n\nThe existing D2 graph and `OrderedSourceLoader` now drive ordered continuation\non every Collection graph turn, matching the Effect runner's existing schedule.\nThe Collection builder keeps one loader set per sync run and fences queued jobs\nby sync generation. The transaction scheduler already deduplicates and clears\njobs, so the builder's second per-context callback registry and clear listener\nwere removed. Collection and Effect also use one dependency-graph scheduling\nhelper rather than separate transaction scheduling implementations. The source\nloader's fixed-point latches still bound requests when a graph turn receives\nno new rows. Effect's immediate and held delta classification now share one\nclassifier; only ordered Effects pass callback-visible row state. A single\nmonotonically increasing demand generation replaces per-plan generation\nbookkeeping.\n\nAgainst merge base `afbeb44eef48d92b6e30ae5fd2843b938ee9163c`, the seven\nchanged production TypeScript files contain 215 added and 331 deleted lines\n(net **−116 raw lines**). Counting nonblank, noncomment diff lines gives 197\nadded and 221 deleted (net **−24 executable lines**). This measure excludes\narchitecture prose, tests, and review records. It is a line-count check, not a\nclaim that each old state was redundant: the joined-source demand and public\npublication gates remain necessary for correctness.\n\nThe full `packages/db/tests` run passed 6,603 tests across 186 files after the\nproduction refactor. The ordered-work oracle passed 93 cells, including the\nunrelated-demand and empty-replay repairs. The scheduler test exercised 81\ncases, including repeated-alias failure ownership across falsy failures.\n\n## Ordered continuation and window follow-up\n\nCode target: `0cbc7ae0` (production change `450b9d3c`). The later CodeRabbit comment at\n`https://github.com/TanStack/db/pull/1909#discussion_r4124008382` identified\nthree additional failure mechanisms. Its suggestion to run the local CodeRabbit\nCLI is separate from those findings.\n\n| ID | Disposition | Public or state-level witness |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| CR2-1 | fixed-now | A bounded ordered repair paused for joined demand and then stopped before it refilled the public window. The ordered-work oracle failed on the missing third row before the fix. An explicit retry also lost its window generation at the same gate. The loader-state test failed on the missing retry request before the fix. Both tests pass with retained continuation and retry intent. |\n| CR2-2 | fixed-now | `setWindow()` settled while an existing joined demand still held a required root continuation. The public ordered-work oracle failed before the fix. It now holds joined and root work separately. Its resolve, failure, retirement, and stale-rejection cells pass. |\n| CR2-3 | fixed-now | Cursor invalidation erased an authoritative repair continuation. A later public-row deletion exposed an underfilled window before the fix. The oracle now observes the refill after joined settlement. Reset and request failure still clear the continuation. |\n| CR2-4 | deferred | The `coderabbit` executable is unavailable locally. The PR check will run on the pushed commit. |\n\nThe follow-up also simplified Effect source ingestion and Collection graph\nscheduling. Collection now checks ordered loaders inside one fixed-point loop.\nA public-query oracle observes a synchronous root refill before its second\njoined demand. Deleting the post-loader graph step made that oracle fail at the\nsecond-demand assertion. This was an assertion failure, not a timeout or setup\nfailure. The step remains in production. The [field-lab loss audit](pr-1909-final-graph-loss-audit.md)\nrecords source-supported distinctions that the short refactor summary omitted.\nIt does not treat those omissions as product regressions.\n\nAgainst merge base `afbeb44eef48d92b6e30ae5fd2843b938ee9163c`, the\nseven production TypeScript files have 318 added and 431 deleted lines. Net\nproduction size is **−113 raw lines**. Excluding blank and comment-only diff\nlines gives 299 added and 303 deleted, or **−4 executable lines**. The DB\noracle campaign passed 2,627 tests across 42 files. The DB runtime suite\npassed 6,609 tests across 186 files with Vitest typechecking disabled. Package\nTypeScript, changed-file ESLint, Prettier, and `git diff --check` passed.\n\n### Oracle guide review for this follow-up\n\n- **ORC-001–003:** The ordered-work oracle names the ordered-window and\n  publication contract, uses an independent filter/sort/window reference, and\n  keeps its grammar, driver, and public checkpoint visible. The loader-state\n  case is a focused state refinement of explicit retry admission.\n- **ORC-004:** This follow-up adds bounded deterministic cells and makes no new\n  generated-grammar claim. The existing generated grammar is unchanged. The\n  new cells reconstruct the named joined-demand, repair, window-move, failure,\n  retirement, and refill witnesses. Multiple simultaneous joined plans and a\n  public-query failed-retry overlap remain outside this coverage, as the\n  coverage map states.\n- **ORC-005–006:** The driver uses public live-query Collections and Effects.\n  It checks rows, requests, window settlement, and callback batches at named\n  checkpoints. The pre-fix runs failed at those observations. The graph-step\n  mutant failed at a public second-demand assertion.\n- **ORC-007:** The existing important generated property runs fixed and random\n  campaigns with direct seed/path replay. These follow-up cells are bounded\n  deterministic controls, so they add no separate generated campaign.\n- **ORC-008–009:** The reference model gains no state or new vocabulary.\n  Window participants in production remain distinct from reference rows.\n- **ORC-010:** Each new async control resolves its held promises during cleanup.\n  `withHistoryCleanup` retains the primary assertion and reports cleanup\n  failures separately.\n- **ORC-011:** No new shared semantic fault hypothesis requires a second\n  formulation in this follow-up. The independent filter/sort/window reference\n  remains the primary result model. Remote relation hints remain a separate\n  feature.\n- **ORC-012:** This versioned section records outcomes for ORC-001 through\n  ORC-011 against the code target and names the remaining in-scope witnesses.\n\nThe repaired claim covers finite local joined-filter windows, the named\njoined-demand and window-move histories, Collection and Effect public rows,\nand the specified settlement checkpoints. It does not claim all legal demand\ninterleavings. The coverage map owns the two remaining witnesses above.\n\n## Shipped-byte consolidation\n\nCode target: `b69c189c`. Three independent scouts inspected graph scheduling,\nordered demand, Effect state, package exports, and dependency drag. They\nmeasured proposed cuts in isolated builds before the final edit. The chosen\nchanges remove two unused scheduler extension paths, two redundant Effect\nfields, and unused Collection scheduler overrides. The core scheduler and D2\ngraph remain necessary for variable query dependencies and coherent\npublication.\n\n`Scheduler.onClear()` had no production caller, and no production dependency\nimplemented `hasPendingGraphRun()`. `Scheduler` is absent from the package root\nexports. The change retires those internal extension contracts. A direct\nunsupported source import can no longer attach a clear listener or provide an\nout-of-queue pending dependency. The scheduler test now checks queued-job\ncancellation, reuse of a cleared context, prerequisite requeue, exact failure\nidentity, and later successful work. Existing Collection and Effect tests still\ncheck coherent public batches, rollback, stale settlement, and loader failure.\nThe architecture now says that only queued jobs block scheduler dependencies.\nThe [second field-lab loss audit](pr-1909-code-weight-loss-audit.md) preserves\nsource details omitted by this short reduction.\n\nThe byte assay built the same package path with Vite 7.3.2 `--minify`, then\nbundled selected ESM exports with esbuild 0.27.7. It used gzip level 9 and\nBrotli quality 11. Every row compares the identical absolute path and toolchain.\nValues are bytes; negative deltas save bytes.\n\n| Consumer import | Change from prior PR head: min / gzip / Brotli | Final PR against merge base: min / gzip / Brotli |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Full `@tanstack/db` index | −484 / −112 / −44 | +93 / −75 / −330 |\n| `createLiveQueryCollection` | −325 / −81 / −113 | +638 / +44 / +19 |\n| `createEffect` | −457 / −97 / −244 | +318 / +104 / −71 |\n| Both query exports | −489 / −106 / +8 | +89 / −58 / +118 |\n\nThe named query imports remain slightly larger than the merge base under some\nbyte measures. The full import has fewer compressed bytes but 93 more minified\nbytes. These receipts do not establish a universal net-negative shipped size.\n`@tanstack/pacer-lite` contributes no bytes to the measured query imports, so\nthe scouts found no query-path dependency drag to remove. The remaining\noptional scheduler dependency argument saved only 15 gzip bytes in a trial and\nwould retire another internal test contract; it was left intact.\n\nThe DB oracle campaign passed 2,627 tests across 42 files with no type errors.\nThe full DB runtime suite passed 6,605 tests across 186 files with Vitest\ntypechecking disabled. Package TypeScript, changed-file ESLint, Prettier, and\ndiff checks passed. Against the merge base, production TypeScript is **−156\nraw lines** and **−35 nonblank, noncomment lines**. This count includes the\nscheduler file; it excludes tests and documentation.\n\n## Xhigh review of ordered callbacks and code weight\n\nExternal review target: `80421c8`. Evaluation started at `dd10cb4`; the\nverified follow-up is `b445bd13` (production fix `1a1b0952`). The raw review\nnamed two behavior findings, one duplication suggestion, two lower-severity\nfindings, two oracle gaps, and broader preservation/weight claims. Its reported\nfresh-worktree and architecture-reading checks are provenance, not product\nevidence. The entries below retain those claims separately.\n\n| ID | Claim and technical verdict | Disposition, action, and durable destination |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| X1 | Scheduler clear, abort, and generation fencing preserve behavior after removal of pending graph bookkeeping and `onClear`. The scheduler tests and earlier loss audit support the named paths. They do not prove every possible history or absence of an external deep import. | `already-fixed`; no further change. The scheduler tests and [code-weight loss audit](pr-1909-code-weight-loss-audit.md) retain the exact internal behavior removed. |\n| X2 | Removing the `sentChanges > 0` loader guard and always checking `loadMore` is intentional. The architecture assigns fixed-point control to loader latches and requires graph work after synchronous loader writes. | `already-fixed`; the architecture and ordered-work oracle own this boundary and its killed post-loader-step mutant. |\n| X3 | An ordered `skipInitial` Effect completed its initial phase while joined subset demand was pending, then emitted an initial `enter` after demand settled. A controlled child promise reproduced this on `dd10cb4`. | `fixed-now`; `canCompleteInitialLoad` now checks the same joined-work condition as callback flush. The ordered-work oracle holds child demand, asserts no callback before or after settlement, then observes a later update. |\n| X4 | An unprojected order-key change allegedly stopped emitting an `update` because of the classifier merge. The exact public probe emitted **no update** with both current code and the reviewed `80421c8` Effect source. Ordered repair selected callback-visible classification in both versions. The extra `previousValue` claim has no observable event in this trace. | `refuted` for the stated regression; no classifier change. The earlier R2 entry above retains the separate question of whether another valid ordinary ordered history changes event type or `previousValue`. |\n| X5 | Removed `info.sourceId === key` guards were redundant: compiler order info is stored under its own source ID. The review's claim that no other dead parameter exists is an unbounded inventory claim, not established by this check. | `already-fixed` for the guards; compiler construction and ordered-query tests are the durable evidence. No exhaustive dead-code claim is adopted. |\n| X6 | The removed `loadMoreCallbackSymbol` dedup layer is covered by scheduler coalescing and ordered-loader request latches in the named tests. | `already-fixed`; scheduler and ordered-work owners retain the law. |\n| X7 | The classifier merge is claimed behavior-preserving. X4 does not refute it: that alleged old event did not exist. The earlier R2 differential found no divergence in its sampled histories, but no universal equivalence follows. | `deferred`; the earlier R2 entry in this review record owns a still-missing valid divergent history, if one exists. No speculative classifier state was retained. |\n| X8 | Removed `pendingGraphRuns` bookkeeping is claimed safe. This repeats X1's scheduler part. | `duplicate` of X1; its distinct callback-accumulation detail remains in the code-weight loss audit. |\n| X9 | The joined-filter pending predicate is duplicated across Effect and Collection; `hasJoinedFilterWindow` contains only the source-existence half, so “triplicated verbatim” is inaccurate. Effect now reuses one local predicate for flush and initial completion. A shared exported helper increased minified and gzip bytes in the same-path consumer builds, so the cross-engine helper was removed. | `deferred`; this record preserves the remaining cross-engine duplication. Revisit only with a measured byte win or a new semantic reason to share the state-owner boundary. |\n| X10 | A ready transition from any source caused an extra joined-filter graph schedule. A deterministic scheduler spy saw two calls for root readiness with the old condition and one with the source identity guard. Joined-source readiness still wakes the gate. | `fixed-now`; ordered-work oracle work-counter cell rejects the broad-condition mutant at its call-count assertion. |\n| X11 | `failDemand` leaves an active plan unsettled after rejection. Source inspection confirms that state, but `transitionToError` also makes the live query terminal, and sync teardown clears the plan. Marking the plan settled alone would not guarantee future recovery because subscription status is another pending-work input. | `deferred`; [coverage map](../oracle-coverage.md) names the missing nonterminal per-demand recovery contract and its required plan/subscription reconciliation. No current public wedge was reproduced. |\n| X12 | The oracle lacked `skipInitial` crossed with pending joined demand. | `fixed-now`, linked to X3; the ordered-work oracle now contains the held-demand witness. |\n| X13 | The oracle lacked an unprojected order-key reorder callback check. The review's expected `update` conflicts with the reviewed Effect path and the architecture's held-repair value rule. | `design-decision`; if position-only changes should produce callbacks, define that public policy first and give the ordered-work owner an independent event law. No present regression is claimed. |\n\n### RED/GREEN and byte evidence\n\nBefore the fix, the new public Effect witness received `enter` after the held\nchild load resolved. With `1a1b0952`, it received no initial callback and did\nreceive a later `update`. The ready-work witness observed two root-ready graph\nschedules under a temporary broad-condition mutant; the source-specific guard\nreduced that to one while the joined-ready transition still scheduled its\nwork. Both mutants failed assertions at the intended checkpoints. The\nreorder witness failed its proposed `update` expectation on both `dd10cb4`\nand the reviewed Effect source from `80421c8`; this is a refutation of the\nreview's old-versus-new causal claim, not proof of every callback policy.\n\nThe final DB oracle campaign passed **2,629 tests across 42 files**, with no\nVitest type errors. The focused ordered-work, scheduler, and Effect suites\npassed 250 tests. Package TypeScript, changed-file ESLint, Prettier, and diff\nchecks passed. The test-only cleanup follow-up retained primary assertion\nfailures while releasing held work. The production diff from merge base\n`afbeb44e` remains **−153 raw TypeScript lines**.\n\nIn the Vite 7.3.2 and esbuild 0.27.7 same-path trial, the proposed shared\nhelper cost 37 more minified and 20 more gzip bytes in the full index than\nthe selected local reuse. It cost 108/25 more minified/gzip bytes for the\nCollection import and 38/17 more for the Effect import. It saved 108 Brotli\nbytes in the full index but added 15 and 9 Brotli bytes in the two named\nimports. The selected local reuse plus behavior fixes changed the measured\nconsumer imports against `dd10cb4` as follows:\n\n| Consumer import | Minified | Gzip | Brotli |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Full index | +41 B | +7 B | −74 B |\n| `createLiveQueryCollection` | −65 B | −5 B | +83 B |\n| `createEffect` | +106 B | +18 B | +107 B |\n\nThese are incremental build differences from `dd10cb4`, not a claim that\nevery PR entry point is net smaller than the merge base.\n\n### Oracle guide review\n\n- **ORC-001–003:** The architecture states the `skipInitial` boundary; the\n  ordered-work oracle keeps the contract, plain-result model, scenario grammar,\n  real Collection/Effect driver, and callback checkpoint visible. The work\n  counter is a partial oracle for scheduling calls, not public row truth.\n- **ORC-004 and ORC-007:** These are deterministic controls. The existing\n  generated grammar and fixed/random campaigns are unchanged and both ran in\n  the 2,629-test oracle campaign.\n- **ORC-005–006:** `childLoads > 0` proves the held joined source was reached.\n  The recorded callback list can contain an illegal initial `enter`. The\n  pre-fix run rejected that event; the broad-condition mutant failed the\n  scheduler count after a real root ready transition.\n- **ORC-008–009:** The reference gains no model state. The new checks use the\n  glossary's Collection, joined demand, readiness, and publication terms.\n- **ORC-010:** `withHistoryCleanup` releases the held promise and all\n  Collections while preserving the primary failure and separate cleanup\n  diagnostics.\n- **ORC-011:** No plausible shared semantic fault between the existing plain\n  row reference and the `skipInitial` callback law was named. The independent\n  callback assertion uses the established `skipInitial` contract.\n- **ORC-012:** This versioned section records the two RED controls, the GREEN\n  run, the exact code target, the bounded claim, and the remaining cells.\n  Multiple simultaneous joined plans during a window move and a public-query\n  failed-retry overlap remain in the coverage map. Nonterminal per-demand\n  recovery is a separate undefined contract.\n\n### Final loss audit and reviewer assessment\n\nThe raw review was reread after the fix. Its 13 recorded items reconcile as\n**3 fixed-now + 4 already-fixed + 1 duplicate + 1 refuted + 3 deferred +\n1 design-decision = 13**. The only unresolved behavioral evidence gap is the\nearlier R2 ordinary-classifier concern, now linked from X7; the stated X4\nreorder trace does not supply it. X11 remains a future recovery design issue.\n\nThe reviewer showed useful depth on X3 and X10, including a concrete async\nsequence and a work-specific source comparison. Accuracy and prioritization\nwere mixed: X4's confident regression claim failed on its own old path, X9\noverstated duplication and byte savings, and X11 correctly identified a\nlatent state mismatch without showing a current product failure. The review\nhas moderate signal-to-noise and good test-gap instincts. **Hire for code\nreview with an executable-verification requirement for blocking behavioral\nclaims; do not use this review alone as a merge gate.**\n"}
